but further technical changes are undisclosed.&nbsp;

00:03:33.560 --> 00:03:39.880
But now the interesting training trick of DALL-E&nbsp;
3 that the authors describe is about its training&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.880 --> 00:03:45.440
data. Because what was the problem of DALL-E 2?&nbsp;
It had troubles following the prompts exactly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.440 --> 00:03:50.800
it misunderstands them or misses details. And&nbsp;
this is no wonder because the image-caption&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:56.920
datasets are scraped from the internet so they&nbsp;
contain images and their alt-text as caption.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:56.920 --> 00:04:04.280
Since me and you do not even remember when we last&nbsp;
wrote an extensive and descriptive alt-text, it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:10.120
no wonder that these captions are short, talk&nbsp;
mainly about the image subject but miss out on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:15.320
details about the whole scene, the surroundings,&nbsp;
background and all the tiny details in it. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:15.320 --> 00:04:20.000
because the training data does not have lengthy&nbsp;
and detailed descriptions, the diffusion model&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:26.560
is bad at details because it was never exposed&nbsp;
to them. So the idea here for DALL-E 3 is to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:32.240
take the existing images and generate such&nbsp;
lengthy and detailed captions synthetically&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.240 --> 00:04:38.480
and then train the diffusion model on those.
But how to create these synthetic captions? Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.480 --> 00:04:42.560
by using an image captioner, basically&nbsp;
a transformer language model with CLIP&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.560 --> 00:04:47.600
image embeddings as input. Image captioners&nbsp;
like this one are really good these days,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:47.600 --> 00:04:53.000
but it also reflects the training data for&nbsp;
short and undetailed captions, so it was still&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:57.680
reluctant to describe the details of the image.
So, the authors finetuned the captioner on a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.680 --> 00:05:03.080
small, human written set of elaborate and detailed&nbsp;
captions that describe many things in an image:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:08.080
the subject in detail, then the background and&nbsp;
the whole scene, the colours that objects have,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:12.240
the style of the picture, and so on. With&nbsp;
this image captioner, they recaptioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.240 --> 00:05:18.960
the training dataset of DALL-E 3 and
trained it with 95% synthetic captions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:18.960 --> 00:05:25.640
and 5% actual captions written by humans.
And yes, the authors ablated between 80%,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.640 --> 00:05:32.840
90% and 95% synthetic captions and found that&nbsp;
95% gives the greatest similarity between real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.840 --> 00:05:38.920
captions and the generated image. But what about&nbsp;
98%? We do not see how this would play out.&nbsp;

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:44.800
This simple trick of synthetically&nbsp;
making the data better helped a lot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.800 --> 00:05:50.440
because human annotators preferred DALL-E 3&nbsp;
over Midjourney and Stable Diffusion XL version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.440 --> 00:05:56.320
1 in the author’s human evaluation experiments.
The authors also show with Stable Diffusion-like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.320 --> 00:06:02.600
baseline models that a model trained on synthetic&nbsp;
captions still produces images similar to human&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.600 --> 00:06:08.120
written captions, because here the blue line&nbsp;
of the model trained on synthetic captions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:08.120 --> 00:06:13.720
is not very far away from the yellow line of&nbsp;
a model trained on human generated captions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.720 --> 00:06:19.640
when it comes to the similarity of the generated&nbsp;
image to the real caption. This similarity is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.640 --> 00:06:24.680
computed by the CLIP model by embedding the&nbsp;
generated image through CLIP’s visual branch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:29.400
and embedding the caption through its text&nbsp;
branch. Therefore, it is called CLIP score.&nbsp;

00:06:30.160 --> 00:06:35.600
Also, the model trained on synthetic captions&nbsp;
(blue line) increases the similarity between the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:41.440
generated images and the synthetic captions,&nbsp;
which is expected by design of the training.&nbsp;

